::: {.card title="How to read this page"}

<div class="small" style="max-width:75ch">
<p><b>The statement</b> is the test the course uses. Change a Level 1 choice and the statement changes: a different question is being asked of the data, and two analysts who set it differently were not disagreeing about method. Change a Level 2 choice and only the "measured as" words change; change a Level 3 choice and nothing in the statement changes at all. The Level 1 choices are listed in three groups: the frame set, the scoring rule, the predictor and the adjustment set name the variables; the sample and the weight say whom the question is about; the estimator says how they are compared.</p>
<p><b>The answers to this question</b> shows all the ways the pre-computed grid answered the question the Level 1 choices fix – every combination of the Level 2 and Level 3 options – ranked from most to least claim-supporting. Dark blue dots are significant at the five per cent level, grey ones are not, and the orange ring is the chosen specification. Colour the dots by one choice to find out which of its options sit where.</p>
<p><b>One Level 1 choice at a time</b> shows what each alternative option of a Level 1 choice would do, holding the other five as they are: each dot is the median answer of that question, the orange dots are the chosen question, and the shaded band behind them is where the middle 80% of the current question's own answers lie. A dot outside the band means that switching that one choice moves the answer further than every Level 2 and Level 3 choice together.</p>
<p><b>All the questions</b> places the current question among every question the menu can ask, each summarised by its median answer; the per-item rate is counted with the count it rescales.</p>
<p>The <a href="spec-menu.qmd">specification menu</a> gives, for every choice, how much of the variation in results it carries and how often moving it alone crosses p = .05; the <a href="../companion/weighting.qmd">weighting</a> and <a href="../companion/missingness.qmd">missing-answers</a> pages take two of the Level 2 choices apart.</p>
</div>

